+We construct a set-driven grammar from each finite positive sample and show
+that it reconstructs every target exactly once a finite target-dependent
+characteristic sample has been observed.  An uncapped conservative learner
+therefore identifies the full fixed-$(f,h)$ class in the limit.  Separately,
+for every fixed rule-rank cap $r_{\max}$, the corresponding capped learner
+identifies the rank-$\le r_{\max}$ subclass with polynomial-time updates in
+the accumulated sample.
+
+Quantitatively, for every fixed finite typing, rule-rank-one targets admit
+characteristic data of total length polynomial in grammar size.  At arbitrary
+fixed rank, a polynomial bound in grammar size and rule thickness is obtained
+when the typing factors through a fixed prefix--suffix summary.
+
+We also establish structural separations.  At every fan-out level $m\ge2$,
+the $(1,1)$ boundary typing contains a rank-one target of minimum fan-out $m$
+outside Yoshinaka's class and outside the regular-filter fragment recognized
+by the same typing.  At fan-out two, another finitely typed target cannot be
+represented as a Yoshinaka-substitutable language intersected with any
+target-dependent regular language.  Full yield-type refinement and
+empty-component elimination can increase rule thickness exponentially, and
+for general higher-rank finite typings an original-thickness
+characteristic-data bound remains open.
